to a place that uses this label,

222
00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:23.330
uses this symbol, then we already know,

223
00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:27.160
we already have in our table what
exactly is the correct address.

224
00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:32.090
So that's basically how information is,
about labels, is put into the table and

225
00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:33.772
read from it when needed.

226
00:13:33.772 --> 00:13:38.510
Now, there's one extra complication
that we didn't talk about.

227
00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:40.360
That sometimes we can use,

228
00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:44.790
we can jump into a label before
the label was actually defined.

229
00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:46.620
This is called a forward reference.

230
00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:50.922
So for example, it's very common in,
in programs that I will need to jump,

231
00:13:50.922 --> 00:13:53.050
let's say, into a label called continuing,

232
00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:57.080
cont, before the label actually
occurred in the program.

233
00:13:57.080 --> 00:14:00.670
If that is the case,
when I reach the first jump, when I first,

234
00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:03.490
the, the jump instructions,
it uses a label.

235
00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:07.230
That happens before I've already seen
the place that defines the label

236
00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:12.710
in the label command, and how can I handle
that because it's not in the table yet.

237
00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:14.940
Well, there are two ways to handle it.

238
00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:19.320
One way, which is usually a little bit
more complicated, is to basically remember

239
00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:22.200
that I've seen the labels, and
I don't know where it is yet.

240
00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:23.830
Keep it in a side table.

241
00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:27.010
And when I actually get into
the definition of the correct address,

242
00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:27.650
fix it back.

243
00:14:29.258 --> 00:14:33.870
Another, another option,
which turns out to be sometimes easier,

244
00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:37.670
is that I actually do
everything in two passes.

245
00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:42.210
In the first pass, I read everything
only paying attention to the labels and

246
00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:45.340
remembering where each label refers to.

247
00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:49.680
That, that's when i actually
build the table for the labels.

248
00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:53.570
And only on the second pass do I
actually go and put and convert each and

249
00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:57.300
every label into its correct code,
into its correct address,

250
00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:01.500
that now is already in the table,
because I put it there in the first pass.

251
00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:05.130
And that's a usual,
usually slightly easier to do, but

252
00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:07.440
you may use each one of
these two possibilities.

253
00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:13.851
So now we've finished talking about the
general process of creating an assembler,

254
00:15:13.851 --> 00:15:16.165
the general thing that
an assembler must do.

255
00:15:16.165 --> 00:15:19.679
And what we're going to do in
the next few units is start talking

256
00:15:19.679 --> 00:15:22.534
specifically about the Hack
machine assembler and

257
00:15:22.534 --> 00:15:26.203
talk about the different parts of
how you actually construct it.